{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Eligible studies were full-text original clinical reports enrolling adults with initially unresectable, initially inoperable, or non-upfront-resectable hepatocellular carcinoma who received conversionintent treatment and were subsequently compared according to hepatectomy versus nonhepatectomy management. Eligible reports had to provide extractable comparative time-to-event HRs with 95% CIs for OS, PFS, EFS, DFS, or RFS, provided that the estimate explicitly isolated hepatectomy status. Reports were excluded if they were limited to initially resectable neoadjuvant populations, postoperative adjuvant-only treatment, transplantation-only downstaging, ablation-only conversion, non-original articles, case reports, duplicate cohorts, prognostic-factor-only analyses, or regimen-comparison analyses that did not isolate hepatectomy status. Locoregional, hepatic arterial infusion, systemic, and multimodal conversion-intent regimens were eligible when the report provided an extractable comparative HR for hepatectomy versus non-hepatectomy management. Initially unresectable disease was accepted according to the original study definition, protocol designation, or multidisciplinary treatment context [37].",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies population (adults with initially unresectable/inoperable HCC), intervention/comparator (conversion-intent therapy with subsequent hepatectomy versus non-hepatectomy), eligible outcomes (time-to-event HRs for OS, PFS, EFS, DFS, RFS), eligible regimen types, and explicit exclusions (e.g., neoadjuvant, adjuvant-only, transplantation-only, ablation-only, non-original articles), fulfilling required study-characteristic details used to determine eligibility."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Eligible studies were full-text original clinical reports ... Reports were excluded if they were limited to ... non-original articles, case reports, duplicate cohorts, prognostic-factor-only analyses, or regimen-comparison analyses that did not isolate hepatectomy status.",
      "reason": "The review specifies some report characteristics (must be full-text original clinical reports; excludes non-original articles, case reports, duplicate cohorts), but does not state any restrictions or criteria regarding dissemination year, language, or broader publication/report status (e.g., unpublished data or conference abstracts) in the provided evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No explicit statement distinguishing exclusion because outcomes were not measured from exclusion because outcome results were not reported is present in the provided evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "The target contrast was the endpoint-specific survival association comparing patients who underwent conversion hepatectomy with those managed without hepatectomy after conversionintent therapy. ... Eligible estimates were classified according to the relation between follow-up, surgery assignment, and conversion eligibility. Treatment-initiation-anchored estimates began follow-up at conversion therapy initiation and classified exposure by later receipt of hepatectomy. Conditional estimates began follow-up after response, conversion eligibility, radiologic complete response, or another post-treatment landmark. Time-dependent estimates modeled hepatectomy as a time-updating exposure.",
      "reason": "The review specifies the synthesis groups (hepatectomy vs non-hepatectomy) and links extracted estimates to endpoint-specific comparisons (OS, PFS/EFS) and to classified estimate types (treatment-initiation-anchored, conditional, time-dependent), thereby defining groups used in each synthesis and linking them to the review comparisons."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_21",
      "quote": "The present review addresses this distinction by focusing on comparative survival after conversion or salvage hepatectomy. The pooled associations favored hepatectomy for both OS and PFS/EFS, and E-value analyses suggested that moderate unmeasured confounding alone would be unlikely to fully explain these associations. ... The present review addresses this distinction by focusing on comparative survival after conversion or salvage hepatectomy.",
      "reason": "The authors state the review's focus on comparative survival after conversion/salvage hepatectomy, which provides a rationale for restricting eligibility (e.g., excluding neoadjuvant-only, adjuvant-only, transplantation-only, ablation-only studies) to address the specific question of comparative survival after conversion hepatectomy."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

