      "quote": "Prior reviews of conversion therapy in HCC have mainly addressed whether initially unresectable tumors can be downstaged to resection, focusing on tumor response, conversion-tosurgery rate, resection rate, safety, and perioperative outcomes [5,6]. Less attention has been given to the subsequent clinical decision faced after successful downstaging: whether proceeding to hepatectomy adds survival benefit beyond the prognosis already conferred by treatment response, preserved liver function, and favorable tumor biology. The present review addresses this distinction by focusing on comparative survival after conversion or salvage hepatectomy. The pooled associations favored hepatectomy for both OS and PFS/EFS, and E-value analyses suggested that moderate unmeasured confounding alone would be unlikely to fully explain these associations. However, the magnitude of benefit should not be interpreted as the isolated causal effect of surgery. Patients selected for hepatectomy are those who survive to surgical assessment, respond sufficiently to therapy, maintain adequate liver reserve, and remain technically resectable. Accordingly, the observed survival advantage is best interpreted as the outcome of a successful conversion pathway, in which surgical clearance may contribute to the observed survival association within a highly selected responder population.",
